Third-party crates
------------------
A shipped binary carries third-party crates, and sutura-attribution.md - published with every
release, hashed and signed like every other asset - names every one this workspace resolves
together with the licence it declares. THAT ASSET IS WHERE THIS NOTICE CONTINUES: it is generated
by `cargo metadata` from the Cargo.lock of the tag it accompanies, so it describes the resolution
the release was built from and cannot fall out of step with it.

There is deliberately NO committed copy in this repository. A committed derived file falls behind
Cargo.lock the moment a dependency moves, and the release would then carry a signed statement about
a resolution that no longer existed. `just attribution` writes a copy under target/ for anyone who
wants to read the current list, and `cargo xtask check-attribution-owner` refuses a committed one.

What it does not carry is the notice text of each dependency: an Apache-2.0 crate's own NOTICE file
lives in its source tree rather than in its metadata. The per-release licence review, produced by
the release workflow and signed like every other asset, is the curated second opinion; see
docs/verifying-a-release.md.

Vendored code
-------------
Code derived from an upstream project retains its original licence header and is recorded
in VENDOR.md with the upstream repository, commit, date and the local modifications made.
REUSE.toml records its licence per file. The two crates under vendor/mimalloc_rust are declared as
path dependencies rather than registry ones, and they are named in sutura-attribution.md like any
other third-party crate: what selects a row there is not being a workspace member, never where the
code came from. VENDOR.md and REUSE.toml are their provenance record, not a substitute for
attribution.
